# Paths that must never reach the public repo or a Zenodo release archive.
#
# `export-ignore` excludes a path from `git archive`, which is both how
# publish.sh builds the public tree AND how GitHub generates the "Source
# code (tar.gz / zip)" assets that Zenodo archives for a Release. So these
# rules do double duty: they keep private files out of island-vital-signs,
# and they act as a backstop inside island-vital-signs if something private
# is ever committed there by mistake.
#
# This file is deliberately NOT export-ignore'd, so it travels to the public
# repo and keeps protecting the Zenodo archive there too.

# --- Development pipeline and notes: private repo only ---
data-prep/       export-ignore
claude-helper/   export-ignore
CLAUDE           export-ignore
.claude/         export-ignore
package-backups/ export-ignore

# --- Local R session / environment: must never be published ---
# .Rprofile sources renv/activate.R. On a Posit Connect deploy that tries to
# bootstrap renv against a project library that does not exist on the server,
# which breaks app startup. Keep it local.
.Rprofile        export-ignore
.Rproj.user/     export-ignore
*.Rproj          export-ignore
Rplots.pdf       export-ignore

# --- Data: lives in its own Zenodo deposit, not in the code repo ---
data/            export-ignore
data.zip         export-ignore

# --- Release tooling itself ---
publish.sh       export-ignore
PUBLISHING.md    export-ignore

# Shell scripts must keep LF endings. core.autocrlf would otherwise rewrite
# publish.sh with CRLF on checkout, and bash then fails on the stray carriage
# returns with "$'\r': command not found".
*.sh             text eol=lf
